mruby gap: bare `module_function` (the "every method defined from here on
in this module body becomes a module function" scope form, called with no
arguments) is a literal no-op in vendored mruby -- src/class.c's own
mrb_mod_module_function: `if (argc == 0) { /* set MODFUNC SCOPE if
implemented */ return mod; }`, explicitly unimplemented upstream. The
explicit form (`module_function :name, ...`, naming methods already
defined above it) works fine and is what this patch switches to.

`ruby -e 'module Foo; module_function; def bar(x); x*2; end; end; p
Foo.bar(3)'` returns 6 in CRuby; the same script under vendored mruby
raises `NoMethodError: undefined method 'bar' for Module`.

optcarrot (see tools/optcarrot_probe/README.md) uses the bare form in
three files -- lib/optcarrot/driver.rb, lib/optcarrot/palette.rb, and
lib/optcarrot/driver/misc.rb -- each moved here to the explicit
end-of-module form, naming exactly the methods each module already
defines above it. No behavioral difference in CRuby (verified via
optcarrot's own checksum output matching unpatched CRuby exactly), and
this is optcarrot's own upstream source being patched (not vendored
mruby, which this project does not control the compiler-bug/gap fixes
for the way patches/mruby-*.patch do) -- so this patch lives on the
3rd/optcarrot submodule checkout instead, applied the same way and with
the same script (scripts/apply_mruby_patch.bash) as those.

